- [x] Extend the runtime fixture to drive `LumOnRenderer` and the world-probe update renderer alongside the production geometry/cache owners through registered callbacks and real provider wiring. Include production anchor generation, tracing, temporal processing, filtering, gather and final output.
- [x] Exercise actual worker scheduling, delayed CPU completion, pending GPU queries and bounded upload admission. Invalidate or replace dependencies while work is pending; prove obsolete results cannot repopulate cleared atlases or final pixels.
- [x] Run illumination/removal/restoration, resource recreation, world teardown and restart through the composed runtime. Verify callback ordering, history/atlas resets, republishing and finite final output without OpenGL errors.

Completion: the runtime owns consumer scheduling and invalidation end to end; manually calling the integrator, resolver and uploader in test order is supplementary evidence only.

Traceability and verification:

| Task | Controlling document sections | Requirements and evidence |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Composed consumers | LumOn.SurfaceCache.LightingContract.md: Geometry-hit consumers; Atlas and publication design | Registered cache, LumOnRenderer and world-probe update callbacks, actual providers, generated anchors and real shader passes; read production full-resolution indirect output and worker-owned world atlas in both gather modes. |
| Pending work and admission | LumOn.SurfaceCache.LightingContract.md: Geometry-hit consumers | Hold real worker traversal at the engine accessor boundary; observe real queued GPU hit queries; change dependencies before completion; admit only complete probe uploads inside the byte cap; prove stale results cannot restore illumination. |
| Lifetime transitions | LumOn.SurfaceCache.LightingContract.md: Indirect estimator and history; Atlas and publication design | Source off/on, cache and probe-resource replacement, world teardown/restart, callback ordering and disposal; retained state must reset and recover without GL errors. |

Acceptance bounds: at most 160 registered frames per settled transition, eight screen directions per probe per frame, one world probe with 64 directions and one whole-probe upload per frame (1,576 bytes). A below-one-probe budget must leave the world atlas untouched. RGB darkness tolerance is 0.0001, lit output exceeds 0.001, and every observed output must be finite. Terrain raster/voxel inputs are controlled; no cache or probe radiance is injected. The final consumer output here is LumOnRenderer's production full-resolution indirect texture handed to composition; Section 13 separately covers PBR composition and its numerical accounting. The 2x2 raster uses one production-generated anchor per pixel, keeping stationary orchestration checks separate from the spatial interpolation and movement scenarios in Section 11. Pending-work scenarios first establish bright world radiance, then invalidate dependencies while an actual CPU worker or GPU query is pending. Upload admission uses 1,575 bytes to reject a 1,576-byte whole probe and checks untouched RGBA and confidence metadata before restoring the full budget.

Observed coverage: `SurfaceLightingConsumerRuntimeTests` adds six GPU cases: the lifetime scenario in both gather modes, whole-probe upload admission, invalidation of pending GPU queries, invalidation of delayed worker completion, and callback disposal. The composed fixture registers the actual producer and consumer owners and supplies their real providers; only engine camera, terrain raster and voxel inputs are controlled. Production temporal/filter outputs and generated anchors are checked alongside the world atlas and full-resolution indirect output.

The lifetime scenario reproduced stale final indirect lighting after LeaveWorld: probe history was zero but the final output retained RGB energy (14.125 in the failing SH case). `LumOnBufferManager.ClearHistory` now also clears both indirect outputs. Both consumer renderers now unregister their callbacks on disposal. They reuse the existing camera snapshot abstraction for controlled engine input while retaining the default engine camera source and coordinate math. No lighting shader was changed.

Validation: the normal shader-enabled application/test build passed and verified all binaries/contracts current. The final GPU run passed 26/26 with zero failures or skips: composed consumers 6, existing cache runtime 5, persistent temporal history 10, world-probe flow 5. A subsequent latest-source rerun passed both lifetime cases with the tightened 0.0001 darkness tolerance (2/2, no skips). Receipts: `artifacts/surface-runtime-consumers/runtime-final-and-neighbors.{log,trx}` and `runtime-final-tolerance.{log,trx}`. Earlier failing receipts remain available, including `runtime-modloader.{log,trx}` for the stale final-output reproduction. Five existing unrelated test-analyzer warnings remain. Builds used isolated outputs without replacing the running game's files. This is executed headless GPU evidence, not live in-game visual verification.

Second implementation review and independent completion audit passed with no outstanding required findings. All three tasks are complete.

## 11. Movement, streaming and mixed probe contribution

- [x] Move and rotate the controlled camera, including view bobbing, while observing fixed world geometry. Verify lighting and debug-independent final output stay aligned with world surfaces using corresponding world positions or a defined reference view.
- [x] Cross positive and negative chunk boundaries, wrap geometry/probe storage, unload/reload cells and move cache coverage. Verify overlap continuity, rejection of reused stale slots and bounded recovery after newly resident data publishes.
- [x] Enable screen and world probes together, including gather fallback. Transition geometry between visible and offscreen states and vary screen-probe confidence through actual tracing/availability changes; verify contribution follows the documented confidence policy without unexplained loss or double counting.
- [x] Use bright and dark neighboring regions separated by a wall to check per-pixel leakage and visibility discontinuities during movement, streaming and confidence transitions in both gather modes.

Completion: moving, mixed-consumer tests assert spatial correspondence and transition behavior; stationary isolated-path energy checks do not satisfy this section.

Traceability and executed verification:

| Task | Controlling document sections | Requirements and evidence |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Moving camera | LumOn.SurfaceCache.LightingContract.md: Geometry-hit consumers | `MovingCameraKeepsAnchorsOnWorldSurfaces` drives camera translation/yaw/bob with retained temporal processing, independent ray/box raster and per-pixel world-position/normal checks; both gather modes. |
| Streaming/storage reuse | LumOn.SurfaceCache.LightingContract.md: Atlas and publication design; Geometry-hit consumers | `ProbeRingPreservesOverlapWithinStableGeometryCoverage` checks exact known overlapping tiles and reference-view return. `SignedStreamingRejectsReusedUnavailableSlots` checks signed crossings, retained allocations, wrapped readiness, a reused physical cache slot with a changed generation, unloaded identities and bounded recovery. |
| Mixed contribution | LumOn.SurfaceCache.LightingContract.md: Geometry-hit consumers | `MixedConsumersFollowReplacementPolicy` uses camera rotation and real geometry unavailability. Paired production output checks no added world term on supported screen results, directional world tracing and isolated gather replacement; all lit pixels must remain lit. |
| Wall-separated lighting | LumOn.SurfaceCache.LightingContract.md: Supported light adaptation; Geometry-hit consumers | `SealedDarkNeighborsRemainDarkWhileMoving` checks every RGB channel immediately and after settling across movement, signed streaming and geometry unavailability; source fields remain independent on opposite sides of the wall. |

Acceptance bounds: at most 160 registered frames per transition. Camera movement uses eight screen directions per frame, temporal alpha 0.9 and velocity reprojection; the other spatial cases use full 64-direction updates and temporal alpha zero to isolate storage/interpolation. World probes use 64 directions, at most eight probe admissions per frame and 12,608 upload bytes. RGB dark tolerance is 0.0001, every lit final RGB channel exceeds 0.001, all observations are finite. Generated anchors must match independently intersected fixed world surfaces within 0.02 blocks and world normals within 0.001. Returning to the same reference eye/origin after probe-ring movement must reproduce every RGB channel within 0.03. Ring-overlap RGBA retention is exact for every texel of all four surviving tiles; the producer is frozen after all room pages are ready and upload admission is held below one complete probe while observing the ring shift, isolating retention from normal asynchronous refresh. Cache slot reuse must change its generation before unavailable source data can publish. Source geometry/raster/availability are controlled, not downstream lighting. The production full-resolution indirect output is observed; PBR composition remains Section 13.

Implementation findings: the moving multi-slot fixture exposed a CPU/shader chunk-slot stride mismatch (32 versus 16 bytes); the CPU record now matches the shaders' `ivec4[]` layout, guarded by `ChunkSlotsMatchShaderIvec4ArrayStride`. The mixed comparison exposed the missing SH9 gather suppression assignment. Direct atlas gather now clamps tile coordinates consistently with its clamped anchors. Finally, GPU readback measured a receiver approximately 0.000006 blocks inside its voxel face, causing a coplanar world-probe visibility ray to hit immediately; gather applies the fixed 0.001-block visible-normal offset documented in the lighting contract. Four focused endpoint cases guard both reconstruction tolerance and rejection farther inside the wall. Temporary shader diagnostics were removed. The broader regression also exposed a test-isolation defect: the static test uniform ring retained a buffer name and persistent mapping after its GL context was destroyed. The headless fixture now releases that test-owned ring while its context is current, before teardown. `TestUniformRing` in the test project owns allocation and cleanup through the existing production allocator API; production classes contain no ring test helpers. Pending-query inspection also belongs to the runtime test fixture, not the world-probe renderer. Normal rendering lifetime is unchanged. This ownership cleanup passed the normal shader-enabled build and 39/39 cross-collection regression cases with zero skips: `artifacts/surface-spatial/test-owner-cleanup.{log,trx}` (18 direct gather, six consumer runtime, five cache runtime and ten spatial runtime).
Validation: the final shader-enabled application/test build passed, and the combined latest-source regression passed 57/57 with zero skips: spatial runtime 10, existing consumer runtime 6, cache runtime 5, persistent temporal history 10, world-probe flow 5, direct gather 18 and CPU/GPU layouts 3. Receipt: `artifacts/surface-spatial/spatial-final-isolated-context.{log,trx}`. The visibility run passed 86/86 with zero skips (76 direct-visibility cases, including four new endpoint cases, plus the ten spatial cases), with a fresh compile of all 100 stages / 245 variants: `spatial-endpoints.{log,trx}` in the same directory. These runs cover 133 distinct cases. Five existing unrelated test-analyzer warnings remain; these are headless GPU results, not live in-game verification.

The earlier combined regression's cache/consumer failures passed in isolated processes and then together in the single-context 31/31 run (`spatial-one-collection.{log,trx}`). The final combined 57/57 run verifies the test-context cleanup correction. Exact ring retention additionally passed 2/2 with upload admission paused (`spatial-ring-final.{log,trx}`). Initial failure and temporary diagnostic receipts remain available in the same directory. Second implementation review and the independent completion audit passed with no outstanding findings. All four tasks are complete.

## 12. Migrate broad lighting scenarios to production-driven E2E coverage

Depends on Sections 10–11. Governed by [Surface-cache lighting contract](LumOn.SurfaceCache.LightingContract.md), especially Geometry-hit consumers, Atlas and publication design, and Indirect estimator and history. This section replaces manually assembled lighting pipelines as the basis for broad E2E claims; Section 13 then extends the resulting runtime coverage through PBR composition.

- [ ] Inventory the broad scenarios in `SurfaceLightingFinalPixelsTests`, `SurfaceLightingTemporalHistoryTests`, `SurfaceLightingWorldProbeFlowTests` and their fixtures. Map every existing assertion to a migrated E2E scenario or a deliberately retained component test. Preserve unique lighting, confidence, occlusion, scheduling and lifecycle checks; distinguish shader-chain integration evidence from production-runtime E2E evidence.
- [ ] Consolidate reusable controlled-world and headless engine fixtures around the actual production initialization, registration and provider-wiring paths. Reuse the existing runtime fixture and production owners; replace duplicated shader creation/registration maps and renderer wiring with the production path. Keep scene authorship, engine-service adapters, frame advancement and observations modular. Explicitly document the simulated engine boundary and any bootstrap behavior still outside coverage.
- [ ] Make production systems own intermediate resource allocation, shader selection, parameters and bindings, pass ordering, barriers, worker/query/upload scheduling, history invalidation, buffer swaps, publication and teardown. E2E fixtures may supply engine inputs such as voxels, materials, light fields, camera and terrain raster/feedback, and read produced outputs; they must not fabricate probe anchors or downstream radiance, allocate pass intermediates, or manually dispatch the lighting chain.
- [ ] Migrate the broad source removal/restoration, emission-only, bounce, valid-dark/unavailable, stale-page, doorway and retained-history scenarios onto that shared runtime. Reuse the movement, streaming and mixed-probe scenarios already driven by registered callbacks. Exercise both gather modes, preserve or strengthen existing pixel assertions and explicit update budgets, and retain intermediate readbacks for diagnosis without taking over production execution.
- [ ] Remove superseded pipeline-assembly helpers and repetitive per-pass setup once their coverage is accounted for. Retain manual shader setup only for narrowly scoped component tests with explicit boundaries. Keep test controls and inspection helpers in the test project; add no test-only state, entry points or lifecycle helpers to production classes.
- [ ] Run the normal SPIR-V-enabled build and the migrated suites together with retained component tests across test collections. Verify context/resource cleanup, pending work, restart and binding/parameter propagation through actual production orchestration. Record the scenario mapping, executed receipts and remaining engine/PBR boundaries, then perform a second review and independent completion audit.

Completion: broad E2E scenarios describe controlled engine inputs and observe production-owned results. Their fixtures do not implement a second lighting pipeline or a separate copy of production registration/wiring. Every replaced assertion is accounted for, targeted component coverage remains clearly identified, and no test-only helpers are introduced into production. Full PBR numerical accounting remains Section 13.

## 13. Numerical final-image and PBR composition coverage

Depends on Section 12; extend its shared production-runtime fixture rather than assembling another shader chain.

- [ ] Add analytically controlled scenes with expected per-channel lighting, receiver albedo scaling, metallic rejection and exposure behavior. Assert defined pixel regions and error bounds rather than only the maximum nonzero pixel.
- [ ] Check spatial coverage, localized leakage and channel correctness, and compare both gather modes against their documented integration conventions with explicit approximation tolerances.
- [ ] Extend the real cache-originating chain through the production PBR composition path. Isolate indirect contribution using controlled source changes or a matched disabled-light baseline; verify direct lighting, emission and diffuse material response are accounted for exactly once.
- [ ] Repeat source removal/restoration and history invalidation through PBR composition with retained runtime state. Preserve existing `lumon_combine` coverage and record the numerical and lifecycle evidence for both composition paths.

Completion: tests constrain lighting magnitude and spatial distribution through PBR composition as well as proving that some cache energy reaches a pixel.
